undation (孫少文基金會). It aims to promote a better understanding of Chinese arts and culture. The museum is located at G/F & 1/F, Artisan House, 1 Sai Yuen Lane, Sai Ying Pun, Hong Kong.","wpu":"Sun Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Sun_Museum_01.jpg/960px-Sun_Museum_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kwun Tong Ferry Pier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.306339,"lo":114.221561,"d":778,"ds":"Kwun Tong Ferry Pier (Chinese: 觀塘碼頭) is a ferry pier situated on Hoi Yuen Road, Kwun Tong, Kowloon, Hong Kong. The pier was originally situated at Ngau Tau Kok called \"Ngau Tau Kok Ferry Pier\" (Chinese: 牛頭角碼頭), but during the 1960s, due to reclamation work, the pier was moved to Kwun Tong. The ferry pier complex consists of three piers: a passenger ferry pier, a vehicular ferry pier and a public pier.","wpu":"Kwun Tong Ferry Pier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Kwun_Tong_Ferry_Pier_in_January_2014.jpg/960px-Kwun_Tong_Ferry_Pier_in_January_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Accutone","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.310237,"lo":114.224328,"d":1162,"ds":"Accutone Technologies is Hong Kong manufacturer and market leader of USB and wireless headsets for business and call centers, and premium-grade consumer audio headphones for smartphones and music-playing devices. Initially founded in the United Kingdom, the company later moved to Hong Kong and has operations in Spain, Mexico and production sites in China.","wpu":"Accutone","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d6/Accutone_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Kwun Tong Garden Estate","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.317,"lo":114.219,"d":1326,"ds":"Kwun Tong Garden Estate (Chinese: 觀塘花園大廈), or Garden Estate (Chinese: 花園大廈), is a public housing estate in Ngau Tau Kok, Kwun Tong, Kowloon, Hong Kong, developed by the Hong Kong Housing Society, near the MTR Kwun Tong line's Ngau Tau Kok station. It is the largest Housing Society estate by number of flats (4,921).","wpu":"Kwun Tong Garden Estate","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/HK_KwunTongGardenEstate_HungCheukLau.jpg/960px-HK_KwunTongGardenEstate_HungCheukLau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yue Man Square","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.31361,"lo":114.22464,"d":1383,"ds":"Yue Man Square (Chinese: 裕民坊; Cantonese Yale: Yuhmàhn fōng) is the town centre of Kwun Tong in Kowloon of Hong Kong. It is also the commercial centre in the area.","wpu":"Yue Man Square","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Yue_Man_Square_2006.jpg/960px-Yue_Man_Square_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kwun Tong Promenade","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.3,"lo":114.2,"d":1587,"ds":"Kwun Tong Promenade is an urban waterfront park in Kwun Tong, New Kowloon, Hong Kong. The first and second stages of the promenade, opened in 2010 and 2015 respectively, comprise the first open space project of the Kai Tak Development. The promenade is about one kilometre in length.","wpu":"Kwun Tong Promenade","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Kwun_Tong_Promenade_Lawn_overview_2016.jpg/960px-Kwun_Tong_Promenade_Lawn_overview_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Enterprise Square Five","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.31987,"lo":114.20852,"d":1642,"ds":"Enterprise Square Five (Traditional Chinese: 企業廣場五期) is a shopping mall and office building complex in Kowloon Bay, Hong Kong. It was developed by Kerry Properties. It opened in June 2007.","wpu":"Enterprise Square Five","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Enterprise_Square_Five.jpg/960px-Enterprise_Square_Five.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cha Kwo Ling","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.3037,"lo":114.231,"d":1767,"ds":"Cha Kwo Ling (Chinese: 茶果嶺) is a hill in the eastern New Kowloon area of Hong Kong, and the area around it. It is adjacent to Victoria Harbour and located to the west of Yau Tong and southwest of Lam Tin. Administratively, it belongs to the Kwun Tong District.","wpu":"Cha Kwo Ling","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Lam_Tin_Skyline_2009.jpg/960px-Lam_Tin_Skyline_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ngau Tau Kok","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.321389,"lo":114.218333,"d":1768,"ds":"Ngau Tau Kok (Chinese: 牛頭角) is an area of eastern Kowloon in Hong Kong, in the north of Kwun Tong District east to Kowloon Bay. Largely residential, Ngau Tau Kok has a population in excess of 210,000.","wpu":"Ngau Tau Kok","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Kowloon_Bay_Skyline_1971.jpg/960px-Kowloon_Bay_Skyline_1971.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Quarry Bay Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.2889,"lo":114.2173,"d":1931,"ds":"Quarry Bay Park (Chinese: 鰂魚涌公園) is an urban park located in the Quarry Bay area of Hong Kong Island's Eastern District. It lies between the waterfront and the Tai Koo Shing housing estate, and covers around 9.79 hectares (24.2 acres). The park is managed by the Leisure and Cultural Services Department of the Hong Kong Government.","wpu":"Quarry Bay Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Quarry_Bay_Park_1.JPG/960px-Quarry_Bay_Park_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kowloon Rock","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.316444,"lo":114.198889,"d":1940,"ds":"Kowloon Rock (Chinese: 九龍石) is an island in the middle of Kowloon Bay in Hong Kong, near the runway of the former Kai Tak Airport. Administratively, it is part of Kowloon City District. The island is three metres by two meters in size.","wpu":"Kowloon Rock","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Kowloon_Rock.jpg/960px-Kowloon_Rock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"North Point Ferry Pier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.293889,"lo":114.199722,"d":1992,"ds":"North Point Ferry Pier (Chinese: 北角碼頭) is a ferry pier in North Point, Hong Kong and it is near the site of the former North Point Estate (北角邨). It started operation in 1963. In 1979, a second passenger berth opened that is located west of the older one.","wpu":"North Point Ferry Pier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/North_Point_Ferry_Pier.jpg/960px-North_Point_Ferry_Pier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sau Mau Ping Memorial Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.3182,"lo":114.2288,"d":2039,"ds":"Sau Mau Ping Memorial Park (Chinese: 秀茂坪紀念公園) is a park located in Sau Mau Ping, Kwun Tong District, Kowloon, Hong Kong. The park was built on the hillside between Tsui Ping Road, Hiu Kwong Street and Hiu Ming Street, in memorial to the deceased of the disastrous flooding on 18 June 1972 (also known as June 18 flooding, in Chinese: 六一八水災), which took 71 lives when the hill slope adjacent to the park failed.","wpu":"Sau Mau Ping Memorial Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Sau_Mau_Ping_Memorial_Park.JPG/960px-Sau_Mau_Ping_Memorial_Park.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sceneway Plaza","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.3064,"lo":114.2341,"d":2068,"ds":"Hong Kong Sceneway Plaza (Chinese: 滙景廣場; Jyutping: wui6 ging2 gwong2 coeng4) is a shopping centre in Lam Tin, Kowloon, Hong Kong. It also contains the East Kowloon Office of the Immigration Department. Opened in February 1992, the shopping centre is located adjacent to Lam Tin station of Hong Kong's Mass Transit Railway (MTR).","wpu":"Sceneway Plaza","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Sceneway_Plaza_Void_2015.jpg/960px-Sceneway_Plaza_Void_2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Telford Gardens","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.324722,"lo":114.212222,"d":2089,"ds":"Telford Gardens (Chinese: 德福花園; Cantonese Yale: Dākfūk fāyún) is a private housing estate located above the MTR Kowloon Bay Depot and alongside Kowloon Bay station in Kowloon Bay, Hong Kong. It was jointly built by Hang Lung Properties and Hopewell Holdings. Being the first property development project of the Mass Transit Railway, the estate is now managed by the corporation.","wpu":"Telford Gardens","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Telford_Gardens_201406.jpg/960px-Telford_Gardens_201406.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Chun Chu Temple","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.289417,"lo":114.202889,"d":2169,"ds":"Chun Chu Temple (Chinese: 崇珠佛社; pinyin: chóng zhū fó shè; Jyutping: sung4 zyu1 fat6 se5; Cantonese Yale: sung4 jyu1 fat6 se5) is a Buddhist and Taoist temple located in 1B Kai Yuen Street, North Point, Hong Kong. Chun Chu Temple was constructed in 1955, founded by a community of Hakka and Hainan people living in North Point, including Lam Wing Fai (藍榮輝) and Li Yuk Yuen (李鈺圓).","wpu":"Chun Chu Temple","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Sung_Chu_Temple_08.jpg/960px-Sung_Chu_Temple_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sunbeam Theatre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.291332,"lo":114.200051,"d":2172,"ds":"Sunbeam Theatre (Chinese: 新光戲院) is a landmark theatre in Hong Kong showcasing Cantonese opera. It is located at 423 King's Road, North Point, near the intersection with Shu Kuk Street.","wpu":"Sunbeam Theatre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Sunbeam_Theatre.JPG/960px-Sunbeam_Theatre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"The Harbourfront Landmark","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.303333,"lo":114.193056,"d":2174,"ds":"The Harbourfront Landmark (Chinese: 海名軒; Cantonese Yale: Hói mìhng hīn) is a 70-floor 233 meters tall luxury apartment completed in 2001 located in Hung Hom Bay in the Kowloon Peninsula, Hong Kong. This prime waterfront residence has glass curtain walls and sweeping views of the Victoria Harbour, it consists of 324 residential units with penthouse apartments on the top floors.","wpu":"The Harbourfront Landmark","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/HarbourfrontLandmark_20070724_public.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Taikoo Shing","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.286335,"lo":114.217644,"d":2218,"ds":"Taikoo Shing or Tai Koo Shing (Cantonese pronunciation: [tʰāːi.kǔː sȅŋ]), is a private residential development in Quarry Bay, in the eastern part of Hong Kong Island, Hong Kong. It is a part of Swire's property business, along with Taikoo Place, the adjacent Cityplaza retail and office complex, and EAST, a lifestyle business hotel.","wpu":"Taikoo Shing","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Tai_Koo_Shing_2008.jpg/960px-Tai_Koo_Shing_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"One Island East","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":22.286056,"lo":114.213361,"d":2218,"ds":"One Island East is a skyscraper in Taikoo Place, Quarry Bay, Hong Kong Island, Hong Kong.","wpu":"One Island East","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/One_Island_East_201302.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Hong Kong is a special administrative region of China. Situated on China's southern coast just south of Shenzhen, it consists of Hong Kong Island, Kowloon, and the New Territories. With 7.5 million residents in a 1,114-square-kilometre (430 sq mi) territory, Hong Kong is the fourth-most densely populated region in the world.","wpu":"Hong Kong","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Flag_of_Hong_Kong.svg/960px-Flag_of_Hong_Kong.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}